title: Buses: many wires at once
check: behaves
---- intro
A number needs several wires, one per bit. A group of wires is a **bus**, a `std_logic_vector`. `std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)` is four bits, numbered 3 (the leftmost, worth the most) down to 0.

A gate on a bus works on every bit at once: if `a` and `b` are 4-bit buses, `a and b` is four AND gates side by side. A bus value is written in double quotes: `"1010"`.
---- task
`a` and `b` are 4 bits each. Make `y` light each bit where `a` and `b` are **different**: four XOR gates in one line.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity diff4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    b : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end diff4;

architecture rtl of diff4 is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity diff4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    b : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end diff4;

architecture rtl of diff4 is
begin
    y <= a xor b;
end rtl;
---- hint1
The line looks exactly like the one-bit XOR: VHDL sees that the signals are buses and makes one gate per bit.
---- hint2
`y <= a xor b;`
---- solution
`y <= a xor b;` For `a = "1100"` and `b = "1010"`, `y` is `"0110"`: the two middle bits differ.
---- done
One line, four gates. Click the bits of the inputs on the right to see each XOR work on its own pair.
